    41	## Global Constraints
    42	
    43	- Toolchain and Mathlib v4.30.0-rc2. Do not run `lake update`.
    44	- Committed proofs use no `sorry`, no `admit`, no project axioms and no `native_decide`. A `sorry` body is allowed only between the statement step and the CHECKPOINT of one task. The checks read Lean, not raw text (Astra H06): `lake build --wfail` fails on every `sorry` and `admit` (the warning "declaration uses 'sorry'"), also in an `example`; `scripts/CheckFGAxioms.lean` (Task 3) rejects `sorryAx`, `Lean.ofReduceBool` (from `native_decide`) and every other axiom outside `propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` in every compiled declaration; `scripts/check-fg-syntax.sh` (Task 1) finds the `native_decide` token outside comments, for `example`s, which the environment does not keep. Do not grep for the word `admit`: it matches valid names and comments.
    45	- There is ONE module, `SimplexFG.fj`. There is no variant type, no NJ frame, no NJ bit and no `nonjustifiable`. Do not port a DC declaration whose purpose is NJ.
    46	- Port ONLY DC's static FG (FRAMEWORK-DESIGN §7.1). These DC files are NJ-only and must NOT be ported:
    47	  - `NjGap`, `NamedNjGap`;
    48	  - `RecoveryPrefix`, `RecoveryBoundaryNamed` and the successor frames;
    49	  - `HeightRegimeNamedBase*` and `HeightRegimeNamedBaseDeadline`;
    50	  - `Recovery*` in `DecoupledConsensusProofs/Protocol/ChainState/`.
    51	
    52	  FJ recovery (r148, r151: the reset at M+1, descent, Theorem N/H) has no Lean counterpart and is out of scope.
    53	- The module is FG-only: no SG, no grades, no Goldfish votes as data. Make no claim about "DC with SG under fj" in docstrings, docs or reports. That combination is not checked.
    54	- `FGConfig.timeoutDelay` (δ_t) is a free parameter, and `δ_t = 0` is allowed. No statement may depend on `δ_t > 0`. A research job is checking whether FG-SA needs `δ_t = 2R` at all.
    55	- Spec version. Port the ARCHIVED spec `spec/archive/fgsa-protocol-ae4497ab.tex` (the registry rule: spec pins resolve against `spec/archive/` by hash), except for the ALFS store and frame rules (Tasks 6–7), which follow the archived ALFS spec `spec/archive/fgsa-protocol-183f2c40.tex` (see above). The live `spec/fgsa-protocol.tex` already differs (sha256 `bc093836…`: `R ≥ 2`, the δ_t range, lines moved by +5), and it will change again (r154, FV). Do not port the live file. If you read it, report each difference in a rule that you port, and do not resolve it.
    56	- View-level statements are hypothesis-based. `fjControls`, `fjFrame`, `lexMax` and every view lemma take F as a PARAMETER; `filteredFrom` takes the controls and the block set as PARAMETERS. Every fact about the store or the run (a finalizing carrier in the view, the finalized-crossing law, uniqueness at a height) enters as a hypothesis. Never write "the current accepted F" into a view definition. Only the store definitions and lemmas of Task 7 (`storeControls`, `storeFrame`, `StoreInv` and their lemmas) read `st.F`.
    57	- Frame forms (ALFS). Each frame lemma states its form: (a) `fjControls` (the controls of a view; in ALFS the view is `Σ.T_F`); (b) `filteredFrom c blocks` with arbitrary controls or an arbitrary block set (for example DC's frozen voter tree); (c) `fjFrame`, the special case `blocks = V`; (d) the store form `storeControls` / `storeFrame`. Task 7 and Task 13 list the form of each lemma.
    58	- Collision freedom is an explicit hypothesis: `RootInjectiveOn` (two chains) and `ViewRootInj` (a view and its ancestors). No definition assumes it. `ViewRootInj` is the named idealization of registry §0.4 ("distinct blocks of a view have distinct roots"); the plan's form also covers the ancestors of view blocks, and it agrees with §0.4 on ancestor-closed duty views (Astra re-audit). It is a registered hypothesis of `fj.frame.v1`, `fj.frame.v2`, `fj.frameFeasible.v1` (parts (2) and (3)), `fg.frameCompat.v1`, `fj.inBandLock.v1` and `fj.twoChanges.core.v1`.
    59	- Do not change a pinned statement to make a proof pass. Record coverage CLAUSE BY CLAUSE in `AvailableChainKitFG/Pins.lean` (Task 14): for each clause of the registry entry, the Lean names that prove it and their extra hypotheses, or the registry id, paper step or AP fact that carries it. There is no single `full` tag.
    60	- Use registry ids in Lean names, docstrings and pins. Do not use F-numbers: r148's F3/F4 differ from the blueprint's F3/F4 (FG-COMPOSITION.md, notes before the Index).
    61	- Do not edit `docs/FG-COMPOSITION.md`, `docs/FRAMEWORK-DESIGN.md`, `decoupled-consensus/`, or any existing library of `available-chain`.
    62	- Root imports. Each task that creates a `.lean` file also adds `public import <module>` for it to its library root (`AvailableChainSimplexFG.lean` or `AvailableChainKitFG.lean`) in the same commit. Lake builds a library from its root, so a file that the root does not import is not checked. At each CHECKPOINT, run `comm -23 <(ls AvailableChainKitFG/*.lean AvailableChainSimplexFG/*.lean | sed 's/\.lean$//; s|/|.|g' | sort) <(grep -ho 'import [A-Za-z.]*' AvailableChainKitFG.lean AvailableChainSimplexFG.lean | sed 's/import //' | sort)`. It must print nothing.
    63	- Builds. During the work, build ONLY the two new targets: `lake build --wfail AvailableChainSimplexFG AvailableChainKitFG` (Phase 0 runs in the same directory). At handoff (Task 14), run the full project target `lake build --wfail`. The Mac `lean` has 3 machine-wide slots. Before a build, run `pgrep -fl lean`. If 3 compiles already run, wait.
    64	- Do not add a `lean_exe` target over modules that import Mathlib. Use `lake env lean <file>.lean`.
    65	- Each task ends at a CHECKPOINT:
    66	  - the build is green;
    67	  - `bash scripts/check-fg-syntax.sh` exits 0 (from Task 1);
    68	  - `lake env lean --run scripts/CheckFGAxioms.lean` exits 0 (from Task 3);
    69	  - the root-import check above prints nothing;
    70	  - the task is committed (user decision 2026-09-28: "do commit the work frequently"), with a message `FG module: <task title>`, and only the task's files are staged.
    71	- Prose in comments and docs: ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. Tables: Unicode box drawing in fenced `text` blocks.
    72	- Namespaces: `AvailableChain.SimplexFG` (LM) and `AvailableChain.KitFG` (L5).
    73	- LM files hold definitions and `decide` examples only. Theorems with proofs go in `AvailableChainKitFG`.
    74	- A docstring starts with its pin and clause, ``[pin fj.twoChanges.core.v1] (item 3 (i))``, or with ``[helper]``.
    75	- Port rule:
    76	  - DC's `NamedBlock.node parent slot root votes support rows proposer` becomes `FGBlock.node parent slot root rows`.
    77	  - DC's `NamedAttestation` becomes `Row` (without `round` and `confirmed`).
    78	  - DC keeps an erased twin (`Block`, `CombinedAttestation`); the FG module keeps ONE block type and ONE row type, so a DC lemma about `erase` becomes a lemma about the one type, or it goes away.
    79	  - Put `-- port: DC/<path>:<lines>` above each ported declaration.
    80	  - Port only the local dependencies of a DC proof. Do not copy its broad import closure, and do not port run-specific helpers (for example `namedRootInjectiveOnAncestors_of_collisionFree`, `WholeRunFinalitySafety.lean:97`).
